            "updated": 1789290566682,
            "link": null,
            "locked": false,
            "type": "text",
            "text": "sum of indices 1..3  =  prefix[4] - prefix[1]  =  9 - 3  =  6",
            "fontSize": 17,
            "fontFamily": 1,
            "textAlign": "left",
            "verticalAlign": "top",
            "containerId": null,
            "originalText": "sum of indices 1..3  =  prefix[4] - prefix[1]  =  9 - 3  =  6",
            "autoResize": true,
            "lineHeight": 1.25
          }
        ],
        "files": {}
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Now any range sum is the difference of two entries. To get indices 1 through 3, take the total up to index 4 and subtract the total up to index 1 — what remains is exactly the middle chunk."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "sum(i..j)  =  prefix[j + 1]  -  prefix[i]"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Why the array is one longer"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The prefix array has N + 1 entries, and `prefix[0] = 0` — the sum of nothing."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "That leading zero is not a quirk, it is what removes the special case. Without it, a range starting at index 0 would need its own branch, because there would be no \"total before index 0\" to subtract. With it, the single formula covers every range including that one."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The trade"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Build:** O(N) time, once.",
        "**Each query:** O(1).",
        "**Extra space:** O(N)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "For a single query this loses — you did O(N) work to answer one O(N) question. It pays off from the second query onward, and overwhelmingly by the thousandth."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Where it shows up"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The pattern generalises past sums. Any operation that can be **undone** works: products (if no zeros), XOR, counts of a property. The requirement is an inverse — you must be able to remove the prefix you do not want."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Minimum and maximum do **not** qualify. Knowing the minimum of the first 7 elements and the first 3 tells you nothing about the minimum of elements 3 to 7, because you cannot un-take a minimum."
    }
  ],
